window.GUIDE_INDEX=[{"title": "Models & sample spaces", "href": "models.html", "text": "lecture 1 models & sample spaces home / models & sample spaces 15 quick practice questions on this page try the three-question checkpoints as you read. use hints or retries; viewed solutions are tracked separately. 0 of 15 solved reset this page’s practice 01 / the big picture models & statistical behavior a model is a simplified description of a system. we choose a model according to what we know and what we want to predict. deterministic model once its inputs, parameters, and initial conditions are fixed, the model gives a single outcome. there is no random element inside the model. example: an ideal 1 kω resistor with exactly 5 v across it has current \\(i=v/r=5\\text{ ma}\\). random (stochastic) model the model allows different outcomes and describes their probabilities. it predicts how outcomes are distributed, rather than guaranteeing one result. example: manufactured “1 kω” resistors vary. a randomly selected resistor has an uncertain resistance; a model can describe that variation. these are modeling choices, not permanent labels for a device. a circuit may be modeled deterministically for its nominal response and randomly for noise, component tolerances, or transmission errors. check your understanding deterministic or random? 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. a model uses exactly 12 v and exactly 4 ω to calculate a current. what kind of model is it? answer: deterministic the model gives i = 12/4 = 3 a every time. measurement variation could be added in a separate random model. a sensor model adds a randomly varying noise value to its ideal output. what does it predict? answer: a range of possible readings with probabilities the reading is uncertain, but the model can describe its distribution and the likelihood of different readings. which statements about models are valid? select all that apply. answer: a deterministic model can approximate a real noisy system.; the same device can have deterministic and random models.; a random model can describe long-run patterns. models are chosen for a purpose. ideal circuit behavior can be deterministic while noise is random; random outcomes need not be equally likely. what is statistical behavior? individual results can be unpredictable while the pattern across many trials is comparatively stable. we study frequencies, averages, and variation across repeated observations. \\[f_n(a)=\\frac{n_a}{n}\\] here \\(n\\) is the number of trials, \\(n_a\\) counts how often event \\(a\\) occurred, and \\(f_n(a)\\) is its relative frequency . this observed frequency estimates the model probability \\(p(a)\\). for independent repetitions under the same conditions, the law of large numbers says the relative frequency converges to the event’s probability in a precise probabilistic sense. it does not promise an exact proportion in every batch, or that the estimate improves after every trial. common trap: a fair coin that has landed heads five times is not “due” for tails. if tosses are independent, the next toss still has probability 1/2 of heads. long-run balance does not correct past results. try it: short runs vs long runs simulated independent fair-coin tosses. this is an illustration, not experimental evidence about a physical coin. add 10 tosses add 1,000 tosses reset no tosses yet. the model probability of heads is 0.5. check your understanding observed frequency vs probability 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. a test detects 18 faulty components in 120 trials. what is the observed relative frequency of a fault? answer: 0.15 (15%) the relative frequency is 18/120 = 0.15. this estimates a probability; it does not prove the true fault rate is exactly 0.15. an independent fair coin has just given four tails. what is the probability of heads next? answer: 1/2 the next probability remains 1/2. a short streak does not change the fair-coin model. what can a long sequence of independent, identically distributed trials tell you? select all valid statements. answer: relative frequencies tend to stabilize near their probabilities.; finite samples can still differ from the model probability. the law of large numbers describes convergence. it does not guarantee exact batch proportions or steady improvement after every observation. i can distinguish a model probability from an observed frequency. 02 / the vocabulary random experiments, sample spaces & events a random experiment is a procedure with an uncertain outcome: test three components, transmit a message, or measure a sensor. specify what you record before listing its outcomes. term meaning one die roll outcome \\(\\omega\\) one possible complete result \\(\\omega=4\\) sample space \\(\\omega\\), also \\(s\\) the set of all possible outcomes \\(\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\) event \\(a\\) a collection of outcomes; it occurs if the actual outcome belongs to it “even”: \\(a=\\{2,4,6\\}\\) elementary event an event containing one outcome \\(\\{4\\}\\), rather than the outcome \\(4\\) itself impossible / certain event empty set / entire sample space \\(\\varnothing\\) / \\(\\omega\\) write \\(\\omega\\in a\\) for “outcome ω belongs to a” and \\(a\\subseteq b\\) for “every outcome in a is also in b.” if \\(a\\subseteq b\\), then whenever a happens, b happens too. the notation \\(|a|\\) means the number of elements of a finite set a. three component tests record the results in order, using w = works and f = fails: \\[\\omega=\\{www,wwf,wfw,wff,fww,fwf,ffw,fff\\}.\\] “exactly two work” is \\(\\{wwf,wfw,fww\\}\\). wwf and fww are different outcomes because different components failed. there are \\(2^3=8\\) outcomes, but counting alone does not make them equally likely. check your understanding outcome, sample space, event 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. for one die roll, which expression is the event “less than 3”? answer: {1, 2} both 1 and 2 satisfy “less than 3”, so the event is {1, 2}. two lamps are tested in order. select every outcome where exactly one lamp works (w = works, f = fails). answer: wf; fw wf and fw each contain one working lamp. they are different outcomes because the lamp positions are different. how many outcomes are in the impossible event? answer: 0 the empty set contains no outcomes. its size and its probability are both zero, though size and probability are different concepts. the same system can have different sample spaces if you record only the number of working components, the space becomes \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\). you have discarded position information. even if the eight ordered w/f strings were equally likely, these four counts would not be: three strings give the count 2, while only one gives the count 3. check your understanding what are you recording? 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. four switches can each be on or off. if you record the ordered switch states, how many outcomes are possible? answer: 16 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 ordered state strings. for those four switches, how many different outcomes are possible if you record only the number of on switches? answer: 5 the count can be 0, 1, 2, 3, or 4, so the reduced sample space has five outcomes. suppose all sixteen switch-state strings are equally likely. are the five on-counts equally likely? answer: no: different counts correspond to different numbers of strings there is one all-off string, but four strings with exactly one on switch. collapsing outcomes does not preserve equal probabilities. discrete vs continuous finite: a sensor reporting whole degrees from 0 to 100 has \\(\\omega=\\{0,1,\\ldots,100\\}\\), with 101 possibilities. countably infinite: the trial number of the first success can be \\(\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}\\), assuming success eventually occurs with probability 1. continuous: an ideal sensor reporting any real temperature from 0 to 100 has \\(\\omega=[0,100]\\), with uncountably many possibilities. in introductory finite models, every subset is an event. in continuous probability, events formally belong to a specified collection of measurable subsets; ordinary intervals and their unions are valid events. counting is not always probability. the formula \\(p(a)=|a|/|\\omega|\\) applies to a finite, equally likely sample space. it does not apply to arbitrary component outcomes or by counting individual points in a continuous interval. check your understanding discrete, continuous, and equally likely 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. a meter reports 0, 0.5, 1.0, …, 5.0 v. how many distinct readings can it report? answer: 11 there are 5/0.5 = 10 increments and 11 readings including both 0 and 5. an ideal meter can report any real voltage between 0 and 5. its sample space is… answer: continuous the space [0, 5] is continuous and uncountable, unlike a meter restricted to fixed decimal steps. when is favorable outcomes / total outcomes a valid probability calculation? select all valid cases. answer: selecting one component uniformly from 20 distinct components; choosing one binary string uniformly from all length-3 strings uniform selection from a finite set supports the count ratio. unequal face probabilities need weights; continuous geometric regions need areas rather than point counts. i can define the experiment, sample space, and an event separately. ← home events & venn diagrams → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Lecture 1"}, {"title": "Events & Venn diagrams", "href": "events.html", "text": "lecture 1 events & venn diagrams home / events & venn diagrams 18 quick practice questions on this page try the three-question checkpoints as you read. use hints or retries; viewed solutions are tracked separately. 0 of 18 solved reset this page’s practice 03 / logic with sets combining events use the same die example throughout: \\(\\omega=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\\), \\(a=\\{2,4,6\\}\\) (even), and \\(b=\\{4,5,6\\}\\) (greater than 3). operation plain english example result union \\(a\\cup b\\) a or b or both; at least one happens \\(\\{2,4,5,6\\}\\) intersection \\(a\\cap b\\) a and b; both happen \\(\\{4,6\\}\\) complement \\(a^c\\), also \\(\\overline a\\) not a; everything in ω outside a \\(\\{1,3,5\\}\\) difference \\(a\\setminus b=a\\cap b^c\\) a happens but b does not \\(\\{2\\}\\) exactly one: \\((a\\cap b^c)\\cup(a^c\\cap b)\\) a or b, excluding their overlap \\(\\{2,5\\}\\) complement is the mathematical spelling (not “compliment”). it always depends on the sample space: the complement of \\([20,50]\\) within \\([0,100]\\) is \\([0,20)\\cup(50,100]\\), not every real number outside that interval. check your understanding build the event 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. let a = {1, 3, 5} and b = {3, 4, 5}. select every element of a ∪ b. answer: 1; 3; 4; 5 a ∪ b = {1, 3, 4, 5}. shared elements appear only once in a set. with a = {1, 3, 5} and b = {3, 4, 5}, select every element of a ∩ b. answer: 3; 5 a ∩ b = {3, 5}. the element 1 is only in a, and 4 only in b. in ω = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, let a = {1, 3, 5}. select every element of aᶜ. answer: 2; 4; 6 aᶜ = {2, 4, 6}. a complement is relative to the chosen sample space. mutually exclusive events a and b are mutually exclusive (disjoint) when they cannot happen in the same trial: \\[a\\cap b=\\varnothing.\\] for one die roll, “odd” and “even” are disjoint. “even” and “greater than 3” are not. disjoint events need not cover ω: \\(\\{1\\}\\) and \\(\\{2\\}\\) are disjoint but leave out four outcomes. complementary events are both disjoint and collectively exhaustive (their union is ω). exclusive does not mean independent. independence means \\(p(a\\cap b)=p(a)p(b)\\). if a and b are disjoint and both have positive probability, they cannot be independent: observing a rules out b. independence will be developed in a later lecture. check your understanding disjoint is not the same as independent 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. for a single die roll, which pair of events is mutually exclusive? answer: less than 3 and greater than 4 {1, 2} and {5, 6} are disjoint. the other pairs overlap. events a and b are disjoint and both have positive probability. are they independent? answer: no disjoint events have zero intersection probability, but the product of two positive probabilities is positive. they cannot be independent. which claims are true? answer: complementary events are disjoint.; disjoint events have an empty intersection. a and aᶜ are disjoint, and all disjoint events have empty intersection. they may leave some outcomes uncovered and have different probabilities. probability rules that match the pictures \\[0\\le p(a)\\le1,\\quad p(\\omega)=1,\\quad p(\\varnothing)=0\\] \\[p(a^c)=1-p(a)\\] \\[p(a\\cup b)=p(a)+p(b)-p(a\\cap b)\\] the last subtraction removes the overlap counted once in p(a) and again in p(b). for disjoint events, the overlap is empty, so simply add their probabilities. fair-die example \\(p(a)=3/6\\), \\(p(b)=3/6\\), and \\(p(a\\cap b)=2/6\\). therefore \\(p(a\\cup b)=3/6+3/6-2/6=4/6\\), matching the four outcomes in the union. check your understanding subtract the overlap 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. p(a) = 0.45, p(b) = 0.35, and p(a ∩ b) = 0.15. find p(a ∪ b). answer: 0.65 0.45 + 0.35 − 0.15 = 0.65. the overlap had been included in both individual probabilities. a and b are disjoint, with probabilities 0.2 and 0.3. what is the probability of neither event? answer: 0.5 the union is 0.2 + 0.3 = 0.5. neither has probability 1 − 0.5 = 0.5. why is p(a) + p(b) sometimes too large for p(a ∪ b)? answer: the overlap has been counted twice the overlap is counted twice in the sum but should appear once in the union. subtract p(a ∩ b). i can translate “or”, “and”, “not”, and “exactly one” into sets. 04 / see the logic venn diagrams & de morgan’s rules a rectangle represents ω. a circle represents an event. points inside both circles form the intersection; points outside both are still possible outcomes in ω. the size of a drawn region does not represent its probability unless explicitly stated. explore the shaded event same die: a = {2, 4, 6}; b = {4, 5, 6}. blue shading marks the selected event. a ∪ b a ∩ b aᶜ a ∖ b (a ∪ b)ᶜ (a ∩ b)ᶜ union of two events shaded regions contain outcomes 2, 4, 5 and 6. ω a b 2 4 6 5 1, 3 a ∪ b = {2, 4, 5, 6}. at least one event occurs. check your understanding read the four venn regions 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. a two-event venn diagram has four regions. which belong to a ∪ b? answer: a only; b only; both a and b the union includes a only, b only, and the overlap. only the outside region is excluded. which regions belong to aᶜ? answer: b only; outside both the complement of a contains b only and the region outside both circles. in an ordinary venn sketch, a larger circle necessarily means a more likely event. answer: false ordinary venn diagrams show set membership. their areas have no probability meaning unless explicitly scaled that way. de morgan’s two rules \\[(a\\cup b)^c=a^c\\cap b^c\\qquad\\text{(neither a nor b)}\\] \\[(a\\cap b)^c=a^c\\cup b^c\\qquad\\text{(not both a and b)}\\] how to remember: move the complement through the brackets, complement each event, and swap ∪ with ∩. negating “at least one” gives “neither.” negating “both” gives “at least one does not.” electronics connection: system failure let a = component 1 works and b = component 2 works. series system: works when \\(a\\cap b\\). it fails when \\((a\\cap b)^c=a^c\\cup b^c\\): at least one component fails. parallel system: works when \\(a\\cup b\\). it fails when \\((a\\cup b)^c=a^c\\cap b^c\\): both fail. these are logical descriptions. they do not require component failures to be independent. check your understanding negate the statement 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. what is the negation of “at least one of a and b occurs”? answer: neither occurs zero can occur. thus (a ∪ b)ᶜ = aᶜ ∩ bᶜ. which regions satisfy “not both a and b”? answer: a only; b only; outside both exclude the overlap and keep all three remaining regions. not both includes the case where neither occurs. a series system works only if both components work. what describes its failure? answer: at least one component fails de morgan gives “component 1 fails or component 2 fails”, including the possibility that both fail. other set rules worth knowing \\[a\\cup b=b\\cup a,\\qquad a\\cap b=b\\cap a\\] \\[a\\cap(b\\cup c)=(a\\cap b)\\cup(a\\cap c)\\] \\[a\\cup(b\\cap c)=(a\\cup b)\\cap(a\\cup c)\\] \\[a\\cup a^c=\\omega,\\quad a\\cap a^c=\\varnothing,\\quad (a^c)^c=a\\] union and intersection are also associative: brackets can be regrouped when the operation stays the same. for many events, de morgan still works: “none of them” is the intersection of all their complements. why does the first de morgan rule work? take any outcome ω. if ω is outside a ∪ b, it is not in a and not in b. it therefore belongs to both aᶜ and bᶜ. the reasoning reverses too, so the two sets contain exactly the same outcomes. check your understanding use the set rules 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. if a ⊆ b, what is a ∪ b? answer: b adding a to b contributes no new outcomes, so the union is b. which expression equals a ∩ (b ∪ c)? answer: (a ∩ b) ∪ (a ∩ c) an outcome must be in a and in at least one of b or c. that is (a ∩ b) ∪ (a ∩ c). which identities always hold? answer: (aᶜ)ᶜ = a; a ∪ aᶜ = ω; a ∪ a = a double complement returns a; an event and its complement cover ω; adding an event to itself changes nothing. a ∩ aᶜ is empty. i can shade each operation and explain both de morgan rules. ← models & sample spaces counting & newton’s symbol → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Lecture 1"}, {"title": "Counting & Newton’s symbol", "href": "counting.html", "text": "lecture 1 counting & newton’s symbol home / counting & newton’s symbol 24 quick practice questions on this page try the three-question checkpoints as you read. use hints or retries; viewed solutions are tracked separately. 0 of 24 solved reset this page’s practice 05 / count once, count correctly combinatorics & permutations combinatorics counts possible arrangements and selections. first decide what makes two outcomes different. two basic counting principles multiplication rule: if a process has stages with \\(n_1,n_2,\\ldots,n_k\\) choices at each stage (the stated number available for every preceding choice), the total is \\(n_1n_2\\cdots n_k\\). three letters from a seven-letter alphabet followed by four digits give \\(7^3\\cdot10^4\\) ids when repetition is allowed. addition rule: if a result belongs to one of several disjoint cases, add the counts for the cases. if cases overlap, separate them or correct the double-counting. the multiplication rule for counting does not require probabilistic independence. without replacement, choices change with each stage, but you can still multiply 8 × 7 × 6 × 5 × 4. check your understanding add alternatives, multiply stages 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. a device comes in 3 colors and 4 sizes. every color has every size. how many color–size choices are there? answer: 12 3 × 4 = 12 combinations of these two design choices. choose exactly one connector: either one of 5 distinct usb connectors or one of 3 distinct coaxial connectors. how many choices? answer: 8 5 + 3 = 8. multiplication would describe choosing one of each type, which is a different task. does the multiplication rule for counting require probabilistic independence? answer: no the counting rule multiplies the number of available choices at successive stages. it does not assume anything about probabilities. factorials \\[n!=n(n-1)\\cdots2\\cdot1,\\qquad 0!=1.\\] for a nonnegative integer n, n! counts ways to arrange n distinct objects in a line. for example, three different components a, b, c have \\(3!=6\\) orders: abc, acb, bac, bca, cab, cba. there is one way to arrange no objects, which explains the convention 0! = 1. check your understanding arrange all the objects 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. five distinct circuit modules are arranged in a line. how many orders are possible? answer: 120 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120. simplify 7! / 5!. answer: 42 7!/5! = (7 × 6 × 5!)/5! = 42. why does 0! equal 1 in counting? answer: there is one empty arrangement there is one empty arrangement. the convention 0! = 1 keeps the counting formulas consistent. which counting model fits? order? repetition? count for k choices from n types/objects typical situation matters allowed \\(n^k\\) k-character code matters not allowed \\(p(n,k)=n!/(n-k)!\\) visit k different checkpoints does not matter not allowed \\(\\binom nk=n!/[k!(n-k)!]\\) select a team of k people does not matter allowed \\(\\binom{n+k-1}{k}\\) select k items from n types with unlimited supply the last row is an optional extension (combinations with repetition). it treats items of the same type as identical. the main list 1 selection problems use distinct objects without replacement. for the without-replacement rows, require 0 ≤ k ≤ n. check your understanding choose a counting model 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. choose 4 students from 9 for a team with no assigned roles. which count fits? answer: c(9, 4) order does not matter and students cannot be repeated, so use c(9, 4). make a three-symbol code from five symbols, with repeats allowed. which count fits? answer: 5³ there are 5³ = 125 ordered codes. repetition is allowed, so the choices do not decrease. from 9 people choose a chair, a secretary, and a treasurer, all different people. which count fits? answer: p(9, 3) role assignments are ordered selections without repetition: 9 × 8 × 7 = p(9, 3). ordered selections without repetition \\[p(n,k)=n(n-1)\\cdots(n-k+1)=\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}.\\] these are often called k-permutations , arrangements, or variations without repetition . some courses reserve “permutations” for arranging all n objects; then the count is n!. the counting logic is the same. drone flight plans — list 1, checkpoint exercise 6 a drone visits five different checkpoints from eight. abcde and bacde are different routes, so order matters. once a checkpoint is used, it cannot be chosen again: \\[p(8,5)=8\\cdot7\\cdot6\\cdot5\\cdot4=6720.\\] using \\(8^5\\) would allow repeat visits. using \\(\\binom85\\) would count sets of checkpoints and ignore the route order. check your understanding fill positions without repetition 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. visit 3 different stations from 7, recording their visit order. how many routes? answer: 210 7 × 6 × 5 = 210 routes. assign 4 distinct modules to 4 of 6 labeled slots, at most one per slot. how many assignments? answer: 360 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 = 360. the module identities distinguish the assignments. why is c(7, 3) too small for ordered three-station routes? answer: it ignores the 3! orders of each chosen group each three-station subset has 3! = 6 orders. p(7, 3) = c(7, 3) × 3!. permutations when some objects are identical if n objects include groups of identical objects of sizes \\(n_1,\\ldots,n_r\\), with their sum equal to n, the number of distinct arrangements is: \\[\\frac{n!}{n_1!n_2!\\cdots n_r!}.\\] for aabc there are \\(4!/2!=12\\) distinct strings: exchanging the two a’s does not create a new string. this is different from choosing freely with repetition, which would allow the number of a’s to change. check your understanding avoid counting swaps of identical objects 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. how many distinct strings can be made by rearranging aab? answer: 3 3!/2! = 3: aab, aba, baa. how many distinct strings can be made by rearranging aabbcc? answer: 90 6!/(2! × 2! × 2!) = 720/8 = 90. which problem uses 6!/(3!2!1!)? answer: arrange a fixed collection of 3 red, 2 blue, and 1 green identical-within-color tokens the formula arranges six tokens with fixed color counts. free repetition permits the counts to vary and needs a different model. i can decide whether order and repetition matter before choosing a formula. 06 / unordered selection newton’s symbol & the binomial theorem newton’s symbol (polish: symbol newtona ) is the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom nk\\), read “n choose k.” it counts k-element subsets of an n-element set of distinct objects. \\[\\boxed{\\binom nk=\\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}}\\qquad 0\\le k\\le n.\\] why divide by k!? the ordered-selection count \\(p(n,k)\\) counts every chosen group k! times, once for each possible order. divide out those rearrangements to count the group once. choose 2 components from a, b, c, d there are 4 × 3 = 12 ordered selections. but ab and ba describe the same selected pair. divide by 2! to get 6 pairs: ab, ac, ad, bc, bd, cd. for list 1’s 12 distinct components, selecting 4 gives \\(\\binom{12}{4}=495\\). selecting exactly 2 of the 5 resistors and 2 of the 4 capacitors gives \\(\\binom52\\binom42=10\\cdot6=60\\). check your understanding count an unordered selection 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. select 2 distinct test points from 7, with no order. how many selections? answer: 21 c(7, 2) = 7 × 6 / 2 = 21. choose 3 distinct components from 8 for a test batch. how many batches? answer: 56 c(8, 3) = (8 × 7 × 6)/(3 × 2 × 1) = 56. why is p(n, k) divided by k! to obtain c(n, k)? answer: each chosen set appears in k! orders all k! rearrangements represent the same unordered selection, so division removes the repeated counting. useful identities \\[\\binom n0=\\binom nn=1,\\qquad \\binom n1=n,\\qquad \\binom nk=\\binom n{n-k}\\] \\[\\binom nk=\\binom{n-1}{k-1}+\\binom{n-1}{k}\\quad(1\\le k\\le n-1).\\] the symmetry identity says choosing the included objects is equivalent to choosing the excluded ones. pascal’s identity splits groups into those that contain a particular object and those that do not. this generates pascal’s triangle : 1 1 1 1 2 1 1 3 3 1 1 4 6 4 1 check your understanding use the identities 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. what is c(9, 0)? answer: 1 the empty subset is a valid, unique selection, so c(9, 0) = 1. use symmetry to find c(10, 8). answer: 45 c(10, 8) = c(10, 2) = 45. pascal’s identity gives c(7, 3) = c(6, 2) + c(6, 3). what is the result? answer: 35 15 + 20 = 35. the cases split selections according to whether a specified object is included. why “binomial”? \\[(a+b)^n=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom nk a^{n-k}b^k.\\] n is a nonnegative integer and k runs from 0 to n. expanding n factors of (a + b), a term with k copies of b is formed by choosing which k factors contribute b. that is why its coefficient is \\(\\binom nk\\). \\[(a+b)^4=a^4+4a^3b+6a^2b^2+4ab^3+b^4.\\] setting a = b = 1 gives \\(\\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom nk=2^n\\): an n-element set has 2ⁿ subsets because each element is either included or excluded. check your understanding coefficients count choices 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. what is the coefficient of a³b² in (a + b)⁵? answer: 10 c(5, 2) = 10. the other three factors contribute a. how many subsets does a six-element set have, including the empty set? answer: 64 2⁶ = 64 subsets. equivalently, sum c(6, k) from k = 0 through 6. which term in (a + b)⁵ corresponds to choosing b from four factors? answer: 5ab⁴ the term is c(5, 4)ab⁴ = 5ab⁴. counting calculator use this to check arithmetic after choosing your model. results are exact whole numbers. available objects n chosen objects k counting model ordered, no repetition unordered, no repetition ordered, repetition allowed p(8, 5) = 6,720 fast decision check: would swapping two selected objects create a different result? if yes, order matters. if not, it is usually a combination. a team with a designated leader has extra structure: select the team, then choose the leader. i can explain n choose k and use it in selection problems. ← events & venn diagrams probability rules → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Lecture 1"}, {"title": "Probability rules", "href": "probability.html", "text": "lecture 2 probability rules home / probability rules 15 quick practice questions on this page try the three-question checkpoints as you read. use hints or retries; viewed solutions are tracked separately. 0 of 15 solved reset this page’s practice lecture 2 / assigning probabilities what makes a probability model valid? events tell us what can happen. probability assigns a number to how likely each event is. a probability function takes an event a and returns p(a). it must obey three basic requirements: nonnegativity: \\(p(a)\\ge0\\). normalization: \\(p(\\omega)=1\\). some outcome in the sample space must occur. additivity: probabilities add for disjoint alternatives. if \\(a_i\\cap a_j=\\varnothing\\) whenever \\(i\\ne j\\), then: \\[p\\!\\left(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}a_i\\right)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}p(a_i).\\] the formal axiom also holds for a countably infinite sequence of pairwise disjoint events. for the finite examples in these photos, the finite version is enough. together, the axioms imply \\(0\\le p(a)\\le1\\) and \\(p(\\varnothing)=0\\). “pairwise disjoint” matters. every pair must have an empty intersection. having only \\(a\\cap b\\cap c=\\varnothing\\) does not justify adding p(a), p(b), and p(c): there may still be pairwise overlaps. from photo 7 : the board’s probability requirements and addition rule. check your understanding check a proposed probability model 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. which weights form a valid model for three exhaustive outcomes? answer: 0.2, 0.3, 0.5 only 0.2, 0.3, 0.5 meets both requirements. the second sums to 1.1; the third includes a negative weight. three mutually exclusive events cover ω. their probabilities are 0.12, 0.53, and p. find p. answer: 0.35 p = 1 − 0.12 − 0.53 = 0.35. which conclusions always follow from the axioms? answer: p(ω) = 1; p(∅) = 0; a ⊆ b implies p(a) ≤ p(b) normalization, the empty-event rule, and monotonicity hold. in continuous models, a nonempty singleton can have probability zero. from individual outcomes to an event in a finite sample space, assign a weight \\(p_i\\ge0\\) to each outcome \\(\\omega_i\\), with \\(\\sum_i p_i=1\\). then add the weights of the outcomes that belong to a: \\[p(a)=\\sum_{\\omega_i\\in a}p_i.\\] if all n outcomes are equally likely, each has weight 1/n, giving \\(p(a)=|a|/n\\). a fair die therefore has \\(p(\\{i\\})=1/6\\) for each face, and \\(p(\\{2,4,6\\})=3/6=1/2\\). “random” on its own does not guarantee equal likelihood. from photo 3 : the fair-die model. check your understanding add outcome weights 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. ω = {a, b, c, d} has probabilities 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4. find p({b, d}). answer: 0.6 0.2 + 0.4 = 0.6. the event contains two outcomes, but its probability is not 2/4 because the outcomes are unequal. using the same four weights, find p({a, c}ᶜ). answer: 0.6 p({a, c}) = 0.1 + 0.3 = 0.4, so its complement has probability 0.6. an event contains half the outcomes in a finite sample space. must it have probability 1/2? answer: no only equal outcome probabilities guarantee the count ratio. otherwise half the outcomes can carry more or less than half the total weight. three rules you will use repeatedly rule formula reason complement \\(p(a^c)=1-p(a)\\) a and not-a are disjoint and cover ω. union \\(p(a\\cup b)=p(a)+p(b)-p(a\\cap b)\\) remove the overlap counted twice. containment \\(a\\subseteq b\\rightarrow p(a)\\le p(b)\\) b contains everything in a, possibly more. for three events, inclusion–exclusion adds the individual probabilities, subtracts the three pairwise overlaps, and adds back the triple overlap: \\[p(a\\cup b\\cup c)=p(a)+p(b)+p(c)\\]\\[{}-p(a\\cap b)-p(a\\cap c)-p(b\\cap c)+p(a\\cap b\\cap c).\\] use the interactive venn diagram if you need to see why overlap correction is necessary. check your understanding combine probabilities carefully 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. p(a) = 0.7 and p(b) = 0.6. what is the smallest possible p(a ∩ b)? answer: 0.3 p(a ∩ b) ≥ p(a) + p(b) − 1 = 0.3. this lower bound is attainable. p(a), p(b), p(c) are 0.3, 0.4, 0.2. pairwise overlaps are 0.1, 0.05, 0.08, and the triple overlap is 0.02. find the union probability. answer: 0.69 0.3 + 0.4 + 0.2 − 0.1 − 0.05 − 0.08 + 0.02 = 0.69. if a ⊆ b and p(a) = 0.4, which value of p(b) is impossible? answer: 0.3 p(b) must be at least 0.4, so 0.3 is impossible. i can check a probability model and choose the correct addition or complement rule. board example / photos 2 and 5 two interviews: “at least one” and “neither” let a mean success in the first interview and b success in the second. the board gives \\(p(a)=0.8\\), \\(p(b)=0.6\\), and \\(p(a\\cap b)=0.5\\). at least one success: \\[p(a\\cup b)=0.8+0.6-0.5=0.9.\\] both interviews unsuccessful: this is the complement of at least one success. \\[p(a^c\\cap b^c)=1-p(a\\cup b)=0.1.\\] to check the whole model, divide it into four disjoint outcomes: outcome probability both successful 0.5 first only 0.8 − 0.5 = 0.3 second only 0.6 − 0.5 = 0.1 neither 0.1 all entries are nonnegative and sum to 1. exactly one success has probability 0.3 + 0.1 = 0.4, which differs from “at least one.” do not replace the given overlap 0.5 with \\(0.8\\times0.6\\). multiplying marginal probabilities would require independence, which is not given and does not hold for these values. see photo 2 · alternate view in photo 5 · practise with exercise 13 check your understanding at least one, exactly one, neither 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. two tests succeed with p(a) = 0.7, p(b) = 0.5, and p(a ∩ b) = 0.4. find the probability of at least one success. answer: 0.8 0.7 + 0.5 − 0.4 = 0.8. for those same two tests, find the probability of exactly one success. answer: 0.4 a only is 0.7 − 0.4 = 0.3; b only is 0.5 − 0.4 = 0.1. total: 0.4. for those same two tests, find the probability of neither success. answer: 0.2 1 − 0.8 = 0.2. this is not the probability of exactly one success. board example / photos 1 and 6 the mechanic: add outcomes or subtract a tail let x be the number of cars serviced in a day. the probability table visible on the board is: x 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 p(x = x) .03 .08 .12 .14 .17 .16 .14 .09 .07 these nine values sum to 1. outcomes such as “x = 7” and “x = 8” cannot occur together, so their probabilities add. \\[p(x<7)=1-p(x\\ge7)=1-0.09-0.07=0.84.\\] you can also add the probabilities for 0 through 6. the complement is shorter because only two values lie outside the event. watch the boundary: “fewer than 7” means 0–6, not 0–7. “at most 7” includes 7 and gives 0.93. “at least 7” includes 7 and gives 0.16. see photo 1 · alternate view in photo 6 apply the complement rule components, pins, and birthdays continue to conditional probability change the group you are counting within check your understanding watch “less than” and “at most” 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. x takes values 0, 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.3. find p(x < 2). answer: 0.3 p(x < 2) = 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.3. for the same distribution, find p(x ≤ 2). answer: 0.7 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.4 = 0.7. equivalently, 1 − p(x = 3). for the same distribution, find p(x ≥ 2). answer: 0.7 0.4 + 0.3 = 0.7. it is the complement of x < 2, not x ≤ 2. compare with the original lecture board · photo 7 nonnegativity, normalization, and addition for mutually exclusive events; poker work is partly visible above. view all eight photos . ← counting & newton’s symbol conditional probability → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Lecture 2"}, {"title": "Conditional probability", "href": "conditional.html", "text": "lecture 2 conditional probability home / conditional probability 12 quick practice questions on this page try the three-question checkpoints as you read. use hints or retries; viewed solutions are tracked separately. 0 of 12 solved reset this page’s practice lecture 2 / new information conditional probability “given b” means you now consider only the outcomes in b. within that smaller group, how often does a also happen? \\[\\boxed{p(a\\mid b)=\\frac{p(a\\cap b)}{p(b)}}\\qquad p(b)>0.\\] read the vertical bar as “given” . the denominator is always the event after the bar. the numerator is the overlap: outcomes satisfying both the condition and the target event. in a finite equally likely sample space, this becomes \\(p(a\\mid b)=|a\\cap b|/|b|\\). the equally likely assumption is needed for the counting version, but not for the probability-ratio definition. three distinctions: \\(p(a\\mid b)\\) is generally different from p(a), \\(p(b\\mid a)\\), and \\(p(a\\cap b)\\). conditioning does not say b causes a or happens earlier in time. it says b is known to be true. the elementary ratio is undefined when p(b) = 0. check your understanding identify the new denominator 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. you know that a selected component failed inspection. in p(sensor | failed), what is the reference group? answer: all failed components restrict attention to failed components, then ask what fraction are sensors. p(a ∩ b) = 0.12 and p(b) = 0.3. find p(a | b). answer: 0.4 0.12/0.3 = 0.4. if p(b) = 0, can the elementary ratio p(a ∩ b)/p(b) define p(a | b)? answer: no, the denominator is zero the elementary conditional-probability formula requires p(b) > 0. more advanced conditional constructions are outside this guide. board example: choose a number from 1 to 9 choose uniformly from \\(\\omega=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}\\). let a = a multiple of 3 and b = an even number. \\[a=\\{3,6,9\\},\\quad b=\\{2,4,6,8\\},\\quad a\\cap b=\\{6\\}.\\] before learning anything three of the nine outcomes are multiples of 3. \\[p(a)=\\frac39=\\frac13.\\] given that the number is even only four candidates remain, and just 6 is a multiple of 3. \\[p(a\\mid b)=\\frac{1/9}{4/9}=\\frac14.\\] reversing the condition gives \\(p(b\\mid a)=1/3\\): among {3, 6, 9}, only one is even. the same overlap has a different denominator. try it: change what you know a always means “multiple of 3.” select the condition b. outlined tiles belong to b; filled tiles belong to a ∩ b. known information no restriction: 1 through 9 the number is even the number is odd the number is greater than 6 the number is a multiple of 3 from photo 1 , photo 6 , and the continuation in photo 4 . check your understanding restrict the candidate outcomes 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. choose uniformly from 1–12. given that the number is even, what is the probability that it is divisible by 3? answer: 1/3 (about 0.3333) the condition leaves {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12}; favorable outcomes are {6, 12}. thus 2/6 = 1/3. choose uniformly from 1–12. given that the number is divisible by 3, what is the probability that it is even? answer: 1/2 among {3, 6, 9, 12}, two are even. the result is 2/4 = 1/2. for a uniform choice from 1–12, let a = divisible by 3 and b = even. which numbers belong to a ∩ b? answer: 6; 12 only 6 and 12 are both even and divisible by 3. i can identify the condition and use it as the denominator. board example / photo 4 defects: probability among a known group let l = a length defect and t = a texture defect. the board gives 10% with a length defect, 5% with a texture defect, and a handwritten overlap that reads 0.8% with both defects. the overlap is read as 0.8%, or 0.008 as a probability. reading it as 8% would be inconsistent: “both defects” cannot be more common than the 5% with a texture defect. the original photo is included so you can compare the handwriting. given that a strip has the wrong length, what is the chance it also has the wrong texture? \\[p(t\\mid l)=\\frac{p(t\\cap l)}{p(l)}=\\frac{0.008}{0.10}=0.08=8\\%.\\] make the denominator tangible: imagine 10,000 strips with these proportions. of 1,000 length-defective strips, 80 also have texture defects. the required fraction is 80/1,000, not 80/10,000. reverse the condition and the result changes: \\(p(l\\mid t)=0.008/0.05=0.16=16\\%\\). among 500 texture-defective strips, those same 80 have a length defect. open photo 4 · practise reading a two-way table check your understanding translate percentages into a conditional chance 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. in a batch, 20% have a length defect and 3% have both length and texture defects. find p(texture defect | length defect). answer: 0.15 (15%) 0.03/0.20 = 0.15. among the length-defective group, 15% also have a texture defect. in that batch, 10% have a texture defect. find p(length defect | texture defect). answer: 0.3 (30%) 0.03/0.10 = 0.30. the reversed condition has a different reference group. which interpretation of “3% have both defects” is correct? answer: 3% of the whole batch have both defects the 3% is a joint probability relative to the whole batch. conditional probabilities divide it by the probability of the known group. useful consequences from “given” back to “both” rearranging the definition gives the general multiplication rule: \\[p(a\\cap b)=p(b)p(a\\mid b)=p(a)p(b\\mid a).\\] use each conditional expression only when its conditioning event has positive probability. you do not need independence for this rule. within a fixed condition, the complement rule also holds: \\[p(a^c\\mid b)=1-p(a\\mid b).\\] it changes the target event, not the condition. in general, \\(p(a\\mid b^c)\\ne1-p(a\\mid b)\\). connection to independence — a preview if learning b leaves the chance of a unchanged, \\(p(a\\mid b)=p(a)\\), then the multiplication rule becomes \\(p(a\\cap b)=p(a)p(b)\\). the product equality is the general definition of independence, including cases with zero probabilities. the photos do not show a full treatment of independence or bayes’ theorem, so their dedicated exercises are reserved for later. check your understanding keep the condition fixed 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. p(b) = 0.4 and p(a | b) = 0.25. find p(a ∩ b). answer: 0.1 p(a ∩ b) = p(b)p(a | b) = 0.4 × 0.25 = 0.1. no independence assumption is needed. p(a | b) = 0.65. find p(aᶜ | b). answer: 0.35 1 − 0.65 = 0.35. this does not give p(a | bᶜ). assume p(a) > 0 and p(b) > 0. which equalities always hold? answer: p(a ∩ b) = p(b)p(a | b); p(aᶜ | b) = 1 − p(a | b); p(a ∩ b) = p(a)p(b | a) both product identities follow from the definition, and complementing the target works within b. the two reversed conditional probabilities need not be equal. a reliable four-step method name the target event and the given event. put the given event after the bar and in the denominator. find the intersection from the data, a set, or a table. divide and check that the result is between 0 and 1. textbook support: montgomery & runger §2.5, printed pp. 31–33 (definition on pdf p. 51); walpole et al. §2.6, from printed p. 62. see reading references . compare with the original lecture board · photo 4 the even-number condition, the conditional formula, and the cloth-defect example. the overlap is read as 0.8%; see the typed note. view all eight photos . ← probability rules poker & repeated ranks → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Lecture 2"}, {"title": "Poker & repeated ranks", "href": "poker.html", "text": "lecture 2 poker & repeated ranks home / poker & repeated ranks 12 quick practice questions on this page try the three-question checkpoints as you read. use hints or retries; viewed solutions are tracked separately. 0 of 12 solved reset this page’s practice lecture 2 / counting equally likely hands poker: exactly one pair or at least one? these sound similar, but count different sets of hands. the key is to describe the hand precisely before multiplying choices. a standard deck has 13 ranks (2–10, j, q, k, a) and 4 suits . a five-card hand is an unordered set of five distinct cards, dealt without replacement. assume all such hands are equally likely: \\[n=\\binom{52}{5}=2{,}598{,}960,\\qquad p(\\text{hand type})=\\frac{\\text{number of hands of that type}}{n}.\\] check your understanding count hands rather than dealing orders 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. why does a five-card hand use c(52, 5), rather than p(52, 5)? answer: the same five cards form the same hand in any order a hand is an unordered subset of distinct cards. p(52, 5) would count each hand 5! times. a miniature deck has 8 distinct cards. how many unordered three-card hands are possible? answer: 56 8 × 7 × 6 / (3 × 2 × 1) = 56. in a standard deck, how many rank choices and suit choices does one card have? answer: 13 ranks and 4 suits thirteen ranks times four suits gives 52 distinct cards. at least one repeated rank count the complement: five different ranks. choose those ranks and then one of four suits for each. \\[n(\\text{all ranks different})=\\binom{13}{5}4^5=1{,}317{,}888.\\]\\[n(\\text{at least one pair of equal ranks})=\\binom{52}{5}-\\binom{13}{5}4^5=1{,}281{,}072.\\] the probability is about 49.2917% . here “at least one pair” means that some two cards share a rank , so it also includes two pairs, three of a kind, full houses, and four of a kind. it is not the poker category called “one pair.” why the tempting board expression overcounts: \\(13\\binom42\\binom{50}{3}\\) chooses a marked pair and then any three remaining cards. a hand with two pairs can be generated by marking either pair; three of a kind contains three possible marked pairs. these unequal multiplicities mean this is not a count of distinct hands. use the complement above. check your understanding at least one repeated rank 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. what is the complement of “some two cards share a rank”? answer: all five ranks are distinct if no two cards share a rank, all five ranks must be distinct. a miniature deck has 5 ranks and 2 suits per rank. how many three-card hands have all ranks distinct? answer: 80 c(5, 3) × 2³ = 10 × 8 = 80. for the same 10-card deck, how many three-card hands have at least one repeated rank? answer: 40 c(10, 3) − 80 = 120 − 80 = 40. there are only two suits, so a triple of one rank is impossible. exactly one pair choose the pair’s rank: 13 ways. choose two suits for that rank: \\(\\binom42=6\\) ways. choose three different other ranks from the remaining twelve: \\(\\binom{12}{3}\\) ways. choose a suit for each of those three ranks: \\(4^3\\) ways. \\[n(\\text{one pair})=13\\binom42\\binom{12}{3}4^3=1{,}098{,}240.\\]\\[p(\\text{one pair})=\\frac{1{,}098{,}240}{2{,}598{,}960}\\approx42.2569\\%.\\] requiring three distinct other ranks prevents a second pair, a triple, or a fourth card of the pair’s rank. we do not multiply by 5!: changing the order of these cards does not create a new hand. from photo 8 and the partial continuation in photo 7 . the typed explanation makes the distinction and double-counting correction explicit. check your understanding prevent extra repeated ranks 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. in a standard-deck hand with exactly one pair, how should the other three ranks be chosen? answer: three different ranks from the twelve remaining ranks choose three distinct ranks from the twelve ranks different from the pair’s rank. from a miniature deck with 5 ranks and 2 suits, how many four-card hands have exactly one pair? answer: 120 5 × c(4, 2) × 2² = 5 × 6 × 4 = 120. if instead you count four-card hands with two pairs in that miniature deck, how many are there? answer: 10 c(5, 2) = 10. the two pair ranks are an unordered selection. i can count exactly one pair and explain why “at least one” is a different event. list 1 / exercise 19 all ten hand categories the one-pair case is directly visible in the photos. completing the remaining categories below extends the same counting method to all of exercise 19. the categories follow the sheet’s definitions and are mutually exclusive. for every row, divide its count by \\(n=\\binom{52}{5}\\). a straight has one of ten rank sequences: a2345, 23456, …, 10jqka. an ace cannot wrap around in a sequence such as qka23. reveal the counts, probabilities, and ranking most → least likely counting expression hands probability high card \\((\\binom{13}{5}-10)(4^5-4)\\) 1,302,540 50.117739% one pair \\(13\\binom42\\binom{12}{3}4^3\\) 1,098,240 42.256903% two pairs \\(\\binom{13}{2}\\binom42^2\\cdot11\\cdot4\\) 123,552 4.753902% three of a kind \\(13\\binom43\\binom{12}{2}4^2\\) 54,912 2.112845% straight (excluding straight flushes) \\(10(4^5-4)\\) 10,200 0.392465% flush (excluding straight flushes) \\(4(\\binom{13}{5}-10)\\) 5,108 0.196540% full house \\(13\\binom43\\cdot12\\binom42\\) 3,744 0.144058% four of a kind \\(13\\cdot48\\) 624 0.024010% straight flush (excluding royal flushes) \\(4\\cdot(10-1)\\) 36 0.001385% royal flush \\(4\\) 4 0.000154% check: the counts add to 2,598,960, so the unrounded probabilities sum to 1. the displayed percentages are rounded. understand the factors two pairs, three of a kind, and full house two pairs: choose two ranks without ordering them; choose two suits for each; choose the fifth card’s rank from the eleven unused ranks and then its suit. using 13 × 12 for the pair ranks would count each hand twice. three of a kind: choose the triple rank and its three suits, then two distinct other ranks and their suits. the two remaining ranks must differ to exclude a full house. full house: the triple and pair play different roles. choose the triple rank and suits, then one of twelve other ranks and two of its suits. there is no division by 2 for these distinct roles. straights, flushes, high cards, and four of a kind straight: for each of ten rank sequences, choose one suit per rank. exclude the four choices where every suit is the same. flush: choose a suit and any five ranks, then exclude the ten straight rank patterns in that suit. this removes all straight flushes, including royals. high card: choose five distinct ranks that do not form a straight, then a suit pattern that is not a flush. pairs are already excluded by distinct ranks. four of a kind: choose the rank; all four suits are forced. the fifth card can be any of the 48 cards of other ranks. straight flush vs royal: there are 40 straight flushes if royals are included. the task lists royal flush separately, so split them into 36 and 4. return to the lecture 2 exercise route · try the updated quiz check your understanding make the categories disjoint 0/3 solved enable javascript to answer these interactive questions. a hand has three queens and two sevens. what is its category? answer: full house a full house has one triple and one pair. the “three of a kind” category requires the other two ranks to differ. the ten standard straight rank patterns occur in four suits. if royal flushes are a separate category, how many straight flushes remain? answer: 36 10 × 4 − 4 = 36 non-royal straight flushes. which statements are true for the task list’s categories? answer: a full house is excluded from exactly one pair.; the ten category counts sum to all five-card hands.; “at least one repeated rank” includes four of a kind. the table partitions all hands into disjoint categories. royals are counted separately; full houses are not exactly one pair; four of a kind does contain a repeated rank. compare with the original lecture board · photo 8 five-card hand counting, the complement method, and the distinction between at least one pair and exactly one pair. view all eight photos . ← conditional probability lecture 2 exercises → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Lecture 2"}, {"title": "Lecture 1 exercises", "href": "practice-1.html", "text": "practice lecture 1 exercises home / lecture 1 exercises 07 / apply it to your course list 1 · lecture 1 exercises attempt each problem on paper before revealing the solution. the summaries below preserve the relevant conditions; the original task list is the reference for the full wording. the pdf skips exercise 5 and labels two different tasks “exercise 6.” they are distinguished below by topic. exercise 7’s wording conflicts with its apparent intended comparison; both readings are explained. exercise 1 · three components: sample space and events hint: preserve the order of the three components. “at least one fails” is easiest as “everything except www.” \\(\\omega=\\{www,wwf,wfw,wff,fww,fwf,ffw,fff\\}\\). \\(a=\\{wwf,wfw,wff,fww,fwf,ffw,fff\\}\\); \\(b=\\{wwf,wfw,fww\\}\\); \\(c=\\{wwf,wff\\}\\). since \\(b\\subseteq a\\), \\(a\\cap b=b\\) and \\(a\\cup b=a\\). finally, \\(a^c=\\{www\\}\\). exercise 2 · four transmitted bits hint: c and e describe reception status, not the original bit values. \\(\\omega=\\{cccc,ccce,ccec,ccee,cecc,cece,ceec,ceee,eccc,ecce,ecec,ecee,eecc,eece,eeec,eeee\\}\\). there are \\(2^4=16\\) outcomes. \\(a=\\omega\\setminus\\{cccc\\}\\) (all 15 strings with at least one e). \\(b=\\{cccc,ccec,cecc,ceec\\}\\) fixes the first and last positions as c. \\(a^c=\\{cccc\\}\\): all four bits are received correctly. an event neither contained in a nor contained in aᶜ is \\(d=\\{cccc,eccc\\}\\). it includes one outcome from each. exercise 3 · sensor resolution and interval endpoints whole degrees: \\(\\{0,1,\\ldots,100\\}\\), 101 outcomes. tenths: \\(\\{0,0.1,0.2,\\ldots,100\\}\\), 1,001 outcomes. any real value: \\([0,100]\\). for \\(a=[20,50]\\), \\(a^c=[0,20)\\cup(50,100]\\): below 20 or above 50, still within 0 to 100. neither 20 nor 50 belongs to the complement. exercise 4 · series and parallel systems use \\(\\omega=\\{ww,wf,fw,ff\\}\\). the component names in the question overlap with the event names; here we describe events in words. series works: {ww}. series fails: {wf, fw, ff}. parallel works: {ww, wf, fw}. parallel fails: {ff}. these match intersection, union, and de morgan’s rules. exercise 6 · design an eight-outcome experiment one valid construction: flip three coins and record their order. \\(\\omega=\\{hhh,hht,hth,htt,thh,tht,tth,ttt\\}\\). choose a = first toss h, b = first toss t, and c = all tosses h. then a = {hhh, hht, hth, htt}; b = {thh, tht, tth, ttt}; c = {hhh}. a and b are disjoint, a ∩ c = {hhh} is nonempty, and aᶜ = b. exercise 6 · drone checkpoints order matters and checkpoints cannot repeat. \\(p(8,5)=8\\cdot7\\cdot6\\cdot5\\cdot4=6720\\). we count ordered visits to five checkpoints; no additional start/end constraints are stated. exercise 7 · robot programs — wording ambiguity the sentence “5 different actions” literally prohibits repetition, making (a) and (b) identical: \\(p(7,5)=2520\\) programs each. if the intended contrast is (a) repetition allowed, (b) no repetition, the answers are \\(7^5=16807\\) and \\(p(7,5)=2520\\). state which interpretation you use; the sheet does not resolve this conflict. exercise 8 · words from a 50-letter alphabet a “word” here means any string, not necessarily a meaningful dictionary word. (a) repetition allowed: \\(50^8=39{,}062{,}500{,}000{,}000\\). (b) twelve distinct letters in order: \\(p(50,12)=50!/38!\\). the factorial ratio is an exact answer; there is no need to expand a huge integer. exercise 9 · a mandatory procedure p five distinct procedures are ordered from ten. (a) choose one of five positions for p, then fill the other four from the nine remaining procedures: \\(5p(9,4)=5\\cdot3024=15120\\). (b) p first: \\(p(9,4)=3024\\). (c) p last: also 3024. (d) p somewhere in the middle means positions 2, 3, or 4: \\(3p(9,4)=9072\\). check: 3024 + 3024 + 9072 = 15120. exercise 10 · select components for testing the 12 physical components are distinct, even when they share a type. (a) \\(\\binom{12}{4}=495\\). (b) select two of five resistors and two of four capacitors: \\(\\binom52\\binom42=60\\). no sensors are selected in (b). exercise 11 · engineering teams and a leader (a) \\(\\binom{14}{5}=2002\\). (b) exactly three mechanical engineers leaves two electrical engineers: \\(\\binom83\\binom62=840\\). (c) at least two electrical engineers: subtract teams with zero or one from all teams: \\[\\binom{14}{5}-\\binom85-\\binom61\\binom84=2002-56-420=1526.\\] for a designated leader, multiply the applicable team count by 5. with no restriction there are \\(2002\\cdot5=10010\\) team + leader assignments. if the final sentence is intended to retain condition (c), there are \\(1526\\cdot5=7630\\); retaining (b) gives 4200. exercise 12 · letters followed by digits the alphabet has seven letters; digits are 0–9. a zero may occur first in the digit block because this is an id. treat (b)–(d) as separate modifications of (a). (a) \\(7^3\\cdot10^4=3430000\\). (b) distinct letters: \\(p(7,3)10^4=2100000\\). (c) distinct digits, letters may repeat: \\(7^3p(10,4)=1728720\\). (d) at least one zero = all digit strings minus strings without zero: \\(7^3(10^4-9^4)=1179577\\). choosing a position for a zero and allowing other zeros would count some ids more than once. continue with lecture 2 exercises 13–21 . math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Practice"}, {"title": "Lecture 2 exercises", "href": "practice-2.html", "text": "practice lecture 2 exercises home / lecture 2 exercises list 1 / lecture 2 practice choose the right probability method these exercises use the probability, counting, complement, and conditional-probability ideas in your photos. each task below states the setup, gives a hint, and keeps the worked answer closed until you are ready. topic exercises start here union, overlap, valid probabilities 13–14 probability rules counting and complements 15, 17–18 counting methods poker 19 (full task) poker counting conditional probability 20 and 21 (students) “given” means a new denominator geometric probability 16 (optional extension) uniform area, explained below the list contains two exercise 21s. the factory version, exercise 22, and exercises 23–25 introduce total probability, bayes’ theorem, or independence; these are not developed in the supplied photos and are left for a later chapter. exercises 13–14 have moved here from the original guide. open the original list at pdf page 3 · earlier exercises 1–12 exercise 13 / probability rules possible overlaps given p(a) = 0.6 and p(b) = 0.5, can the overlap be 0.3? can it be 0.05? what is its smallest possible value? hint: the union cannot have probability greater than 1. show the worked solution · exercise 13 · can these events exist? given p(a) = 0.6 and p(b) = 0.5, intersection probabilities must satisfy: \\[\\max(0,p(a)+p(b)-1)\\le p(a\\cap b)\\le\\min(p(a),p(b)).\\] thus \\(0.1\\le p(a\\cap b)\\le0.5\\). (a) 0.3 is possible: take ten equally likely outcomes, a = {1,2,3,4,5,6} and b = {4,5,6,7,8}. (b) 0.05 is impossible: the union would have probability 1.05. (c) the smallest possible intersection is 0.1, achieved by a = {1,2,3,4,5,6} and b = {6,7,8,9,10}. exercise 14 / probability rules three-event venn regions given p(a) = 0.4, p(b) = 0.7, p(c) = 0.5, pairwise intersections 0.3, 0.2, 0.4 respectively, and triple intersection 0.1: label all eight venn regions, find p(a ∩ b ∩ cᶜ), and check whether the assignments are valid. hint: work outward from the triple intersection. show the worked solution · exercise 14 · fill a three-event venn diagram from the center begin with a ∩ b ∩ c = 0.1. subtract that center from each pairwise intersection, then subtract the included regions from each individual event. disjoint region computed probability a ∩ b ∩ c 0.1 a ∩ b only (outside c) 0.3 − 0.1 = 0.2 a ∩ c only (outside b) 0.2 − 0.1 = 0.1 b ∩ c only (outside a) 0.4 − 0.1 = 0.3 a only 0.4 − 0.2 − 0.1 − 0.1 = 0 b only 0.7 − 0.2 − 0.3 − 0.1 = 0.1 c only 0.5 − 0.1 − 0.3 − 0.1 = 0 outside all three 1 − 0.8 = 0.2 therefore \\(p(a\\cap b\\cap c^c)=0.2\\). these are valid probabilities: all eight disjoint regions are nonnegative and sum to 1. a region may have probability zero. a b c 0 0.1 0.2 0.1 0.3 0.1 0 0.2 ω exercise 15 / component sampling four components from a box of ten three of the ten components are defective. select four simultaneously and uniformly, without replacement. find the chance of (a) no defectives, (b) exactly one defective, and (c) at least two defectives. hint: all four-component subsets are equally likely. select defective and working components separately. show the worked solution the sample-space count is \\(\\binom{10}{4}=210\\). there are 3 defective and 7 working components. if x counts defectives: \\[p(x=k)=\\frac{\\binom3k\\binom7{4-k}}{\\binom{10}{4}},\\qquad k=0,1,2,3.\\] (a) \\(\\binom74/210=35/210=1/6\\approx16.6667\\%\\). (b) \\(\\binom31\\binom73/210=105/210=1/2=50\\%\\). (c) \\(1-p(x=0)-p(x=1)=1-1/6-1/2=1/3\\approx33.3333\\%\\). direct check for (c): \\([\\binom32\\binom72+\\binom33\\binom71]/210=(63+7)/210=1/3\\). four defectives are impossible because the box has only three. this is a hypergeometric counting pattern; you can solve it with combinations without memorizing the distribution name. exercise 16 / optional extension a point in a square select a point uniformly from a square of side 2. a circle of radius 1 is centered inside it. find the chance that the point lies inside the circle. why this is an extension: the photos use finite sample spaces. here there are infinitely many points, so equal likelihood is expressed through area . show the worked solution uniform sampling over the square means probability is proportional to area. \\[p(\\text{inside circle})=\\frac{\\pi(1)^2}{2^2}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\approx0.785398.\\] the probability is about 78.54%. do not divide counts of individual points: both regions contain infinitely many. including or excluding the circular boundary does not change the probability because it has zero area. exercise 17 / pin codes repeated digits: exact vs at least a four-digit pin is chosen uniformly from 0000 through 9999. find the chances of (a) four different digits, (b) exactly two digits being the same, (c) at least two being the same, and (d) no zeros. hint: order matters, leading zeros are allowed, and there are \\(10^4=10000\\) pins. show the worked solution (a) four distinct digits: \\(10\\cdot9\\cdot8\\cdot7/10000=0.504\\). (b) interpreting “exactly two digits are the same” as exactly one pair with the other two digits different from each other and from the pair: \\[\\frac{10\\binom42\\cdot9\\cdot8}{10000}=0.432.\\] choose the repeated digit, its two positions, then two distinct other digits for the remaining positions. if your lecturer instead intends to include two-pair pins such as 1122, add \\(\\binom{10}{2}\\binom42/10000=0.027\\), giving 0.459. the one-pair interpretation is used here. (c) at least one repeated digit is the complement of all distinct: \\(1-0.504=0.496\\). this includes pairs, triples, and four equal digits. (d) with zero excluded, each position has nine choices: \\(9^4/10^4=0.6561\\). check (b) against the exactly-one-pair poker calculation : both require preventing additional repeated values. exercise 18 / birthdays at least two people share a birthday use the exercise’s 365-day model: equally likely days, no leap day, and independent birthdays for the selected people. find the probability for groups of 2, 3, 10, 23, and 50. hint: count the complement, where every birthday is different. we seek any matching pair, not a match with one particular person. show the worked solution \\[p(\\text{at least one match})=1-\\frac{365\\cdot364\\cdots(365-n+1)}{365^n},\\quad 1\\le n\\le365.\\] the first person may have any birthday. to avoid matches, the second has 364 available days, the third 363, and so on. divide the count of distinct birthday assignments by the count of all assignments. people n match probability as a percentage 2 0.002740 0.2740% 3 0.008204 0.8204% 10 0.116948 11.6948% 23 0.507297 50.7297% 50 0.970374 97.0374% for more than 365 people, a match is certain under this model. real birthdays are not perfectly uniform or always independent; these answers belong to the stated simplified model. exercise 19 / poker count and rank all five-card hands use a standard 52-card deck and equally likely five-card hands. calculate each category’s probability and rank the categories by likelihood. hint: the denominator is \\(\\binom{52}{5}\\). categories must not overlap: the sheet excludes royal flushes from “straight flush” and all straight flushes from “straight” and “flush.” open the complete worked solution for exercise 19 → exercise 20 / reverse the condition kings and face cards select one card uniformly from 52 cards. what is (a) the probability of a king given a face card (j, q, k), and (b) the probability of a face card given a king? hint: there are 4 kings and 12 face cards; all kings are face cards. show the worked solution let k = king and f = face card. because \\(k\\subseteq f\\), \\(k\\cap f=k\\). \\[p(k\\mid f)=\\frac{4/52}{12/52}=\\frac13,\\qquad p(f\\mid k)=\\frac{4/52}{4/52}=1.\\] in (a), one-third of the twelve possible face cards are kings. in (b), every one of the four possible kings is a face card. reversing the condition changes the denominator. exercise 21 / students, not the factory task programming and statistics of 100 students, 60 take programming, 40 take statistics, and 25 take both. choose a student uniformly. find (a) p(statistics | programming), (b) p(programming | statistics), and (c) p(statistics | not programming). hint: fill a two-way table first. every row and column total should agree with the problem. show the table and worked solution statistics not statistics total programming 25 35 60 not programming 15 25 40 total 40 60 100 (a) among the 60 programming students, 25 take statistics: \\(25/60=5/12\\approx0.4167\\). (b) among the 40 statistics students, 25 take programming: \\(25/40=5/8=0.625\\). (c) among the 40 students not taking programming, 15 take statistics: \\(15/40=3/8=0.375\\). the denominator in (c) is 40, not 100 or 60. also, \\(p(s\\mid p^c)\\) is not \\(1-p(s\\mid p)\\): the conditioning group changed. math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Practice"}, {"title": "Formula desk", "href": "formulas.html", "text": "revision formula desk home / formula desk 08 / quick revision the formula desk filter formulas relative frequency \\(f_n(a)=n_a/n\\) observed count of a divided by the number of trials; an estimate of p(a). equally likely finite outcomes \\(p(a)=|a|/|\\omega|\\) only when all outcomes in the finite sample space have the same probability. complement / not \\(a^c=\\omega\\setminus a,\\quad p(a^c)=1-p(a)\\) union / inclusive or \\(p(a\\cup b)=p(a)+p(b)-p(a\\cap b)\\) mutually exclusive / disjoint \\(a\\cap b=\\varnothing\\rightarrow p(a\\cup b)=p(a)+p(b)\\) de morgan / neither / not both \\((a\\cup b)^c=a^c\\cap b^c,\\quad(a\\cap b)^c=a^c\\cup b^c\\) factorial / arrange all distinct objects \\(n!=n(n-1)\\cdots1,\\quad0!=1\\) ordered choices / repetition allowed \\(n^k\\) k positions, n choices at each position. permutation / variation / ordered, no repetition \\(p(n,k)=n!/(n-k)!\\) choose k distinct objects in order from n; 0 ≤ k ≤ n. combination / newton’s symbol / unordered, no repetition \\(\\binom nk=n!/[k!(n-k)!]\\) choose a k-element subset of n distinct objects; 0 ≤ k ≤ n. permutation of a multiset / identical objects \\(n!/(n_1!\\cdots n_r!)\\) fixed multiplicities n₁ + ⋯ + nᵣ = n. binomial theorem / newton expansion \\((a+b)^n=\\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom nk a^{n-k}b^k\\) n is a nonnegative integer. probability axioms / pairwise disjoint addition \\(p(\\omega)=1,\\quad p(a)\\ge0,\\quad p(\\bigcup_i a_i)=\\sum_i p(a_i)\\) the addition equality requires pairwise disjoint events; it applies to finite or countable unions. conditional probability / given \\(p(a\\mid b)=\\frac{p(a\\cap b)}{p(b)}\\) require p(b) > 0. the event after the bar is the denominator. multiplication / intersection \\(p(a\\cap b)=p(b)p(a\\mid b)\\) general product rule; it does not require independence. the displayed conditional requires p(b) > 0. conditional complement \\(p(a^c\\mid b)=1-p(a\\mid b)\\) keep the condition b unchanged; require p(b) > 0. three-event inclusion–exclusion \\(p(a\\cup b\\cup c)=p(a)+p(b)+p(c)-p(a\\cap b)-p(a\\cap c)-p(b\\cap c)+p(a\\cap b\\cap c)\\) subtract pairwise overlaps and restore the triple overlap. poker / exactly one pair \\(n=13\\binom42\\binom{12}{3}4^3\\) five-card hands from a 52-card deck. probability = n / c(52,5). poker / at least one repeated rank \\(n=\\binom{52}{5}-\\binom{13}{5}4^5\\) includes pairs, triples, and four of a kind. no formulas match this search. math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Revision"}, {"title": "Self-test quiz", "href": "quiz.html", "text": "revision self-test quiz home / self-test quiz practice your way build a practice set a bank of 136 questions : the original 40 review questions plus the 96 new lesson checks. choose a lecture or topic and a set size. work at your own pace, then retry anything you missed. your first valid check counts toward the score. hints are allowed; revealing a solution never scores a correct first answer. you can keep trying after a mistake. quiz sets are session-only and do not change your lesson-check progress. lecture or topic all topics lecture 1 only lecture 2 only models sample spaces event operations venn & de morgan counting combinations probability rules conditional probability poker counting questions 10 20 all matching questions start a fresh set review this concept ← previous next → retry missed questions show / hide review enable javascript for the interactive quiz. the lessons and worked exercise-list solutions remain readable without it. math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Revision"}, {"title": "Lecture photo gallery", "href": "photos.html", "text": "sources lecture photo gallery home / lecture photo gallery lecture 2 / original source material your eight lecture photos click any photo to open the full-resolution original in a new tab. each caption links to the typed explanation. similar views are retained so you can compare faint or partially covered writing. photo 1 · mechanic table and conditional probability the complement calculation p(x < 7) = 0.84 and the initial 1–9 number example. read the explained notes → photo 2 · two-interview example union and complement: at least one success has probability 0.9; neither has probability 0.1. read the explained notes → photo 3 · complement rule and a fair die a complement proof and equal probability 1/6 for the six die outcomes. read the explained notes → photo 4 · conditional probability and cloth defects the even-number condition, the conditional formula, and the cloth-defect example. the overlap is read as 0.8%; see the typed note. read the explained notes → photo 5 · interviews: a second view another view of the same interview calculation; kept for comparison with photo 2. read the explained notes → photo 6 · mechanic and number example: a second view a clearer alternate view of the table and the unconditional probability of a multiple of 3. read the explained notes → photo 7 · probability axioms and disjoint addition nonnegativity, normalization, and addition for mutually exclusive events; poker work is partly visible above. read the explained notes → photo 8 · poker: total hands and repeated ranks five-card hand counting, the complement method, and the distinction between at least one pair and exactly one pair. read the explained notes → math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Sources"}, {"title": "Reading & scope", "href": "references.html", "text": "sources reading & scope home / reading & scope reading / scope where this guide comes from lecture 1 follows your supplied keywords. lecture 2 follows the eight photos in img/lec2, with clearly marked extensions for the matching task-list questions. exercise summaries and solutions refer to list 1 ; the solutions were prepared for this guide, rather than copied from an official answer key. explanations were checked against the supplied course books and written in study-note form. montgomery & runger · applied statistics and probability for engineers, 7th edition read §1.1.1 for variability (printed p. 3); §2.1 for random experiments, sample spaces, and event operations (pp. 18–23); §2.2 for counting (pp. 23–26); and §§2.3–2.4 for relative frequency, probability rules, and unions (from p. 26). open chapter 2 at pdf page 36 . de morgan’s laws are on printed p. 23 / pdf p. 41; counting formulas on printed pp. 24–25 / pdf pp. 42–43. walpole, myers, myers & ye · probability & statistics for engineers & scientists, 9th edition the file named “503 stat - text book” is this textbook. read §§2.1–2.2 for sample spaces and events (printed pp. 35–42); §2.3 for counting, permutations, and combinations (pp. 44–51); and §§2.4–2.5 for probabilities and additive rules (pp. 52–59). open chapter 2 at pdf page 56 . permutations begin on printed p. 47 / pdf p. 68; combinations on printed p. 50 / pdf p. 71. the books use s for the sample space and a′ for a complement; this guide uses ω and aᶜ. they mean the same things. pdf page numbers count from the beginning of the file and differ from the printed page numbers. newton’s symbol identities and the binomial expansion are additional explanations of your keyword. for lecture 2, see montgomery & runger §2.5 (conditional probability, printed pp. 31–33; definition on pdf p. 51) and walpole et al. §2.6 (from printed p. 62 / pdf p. 83). the course description is reserved for future planning. this guide does not claim that all topics in the books or the later exercises have already been covered in your lectures. lecture 2 source notes all eight originals are included in the photo gallery . repeated views are retained and captioned. the cloth overlap in photo 4 is read as 0.8%; that reading is stated beside the calculation. the poker expression that can double-count hands is discussed and corrected in the typed notes. photos show only part of a lecture, so absent topics are not assumed to have been taught. ready for the next lecture keep new exercise sheets in the project’s lists folder. future topics can be added as separate chapters, using the same topic-page structure. adding a file does not automatically update the guide. math for electronics · lectures 1 & 2 works offline. print expands solutions on this page. sources and scope .", "group": "Sources"}];
